slides=[
 dict(id='opening',label='PlanetScale Postgres on Metal',theme='opening',html='<div class="opening-hero"><div class="cover-brand"><img src="assets/planetscale-white.svg" alt="PlanetScale"></div><h1>Postgres on <span class="accent">Metal</span></h1></div>',notes="0:15. “PlanetScale Postgres on Metal.” Introduce the meeting, then advance to the Nexus to establish the buyer’s priority before discussing the product or benchmarks."),
 dict(id='nexus',label='More resources won’t fix the Postgres bottleneck',theme='nexus',html='<div class="nexus-headline"><h2 class="nexus-meta-title" aria-hidden="false">How <span class="accent">Postgres</span> typically runs and scales in the cloud</h2><h2 class="nexus-challenge-title" aria-hidden="true">More resources won’t fix <span class="accent">the Postgres bottleneck</span></h2></div><div class="nexus-art">'+(R/'assets/nexus.svg').read_text()+'</div>',notes="1:00. Start with the capacity model, before the challenge. “The common cloud Postgres model separates the database server from its storage. As demand grows, the usual knobs are a larger compute instance and a larger storage allocation.” Point to vCPU and RAM inside the Postgres server, then the separate network-attached volume. Advance once: both components expand to show more vCPU, more RAM, and more storage. “That gives you more capacity. But sizing alone does not settle every part of the database experience.” Advance again: the headline changes and the three orange discovery topics appear: Latency, High availability, and Storage I/O. Ask: “Which is creating the most pressure for your team today?” Pause and listen.\n\nEvidence and scope: PlanetScale's March 13, 2025 IO devices and latency article describes network-attached storage as a common cloud default and says most cloud database providers use it. Its July 1, 2025 Benchmarking Postgres article says all of the compared products used network-attached storage underneath. Independent documentation confirms the capacity model for Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, Google Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL, and Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server: configurable compute/memory with separate durable storage, and options to grow storage and tune performance. This is strong support for a common industry pattern, not a census or a universal statement about every provider and tier. The artwork is provider-neutral and deliberately omits AWS/EBS names.\n\nArchitecture boundaries: only the resource-sizing model is visible, not a complete HA deployment. Replicas, zones, backups, caches, storage replication, proxies, and managed-service control planes are omitted for clarity; their absence does not mean the providers lack them. Aurora, AlloyDB, and Neon have different disaggregated storage architectures and may use local caching, so do not describe all of them as an identical block-volume implementation. CPU and RAM often come in machine classes/ratios, while storage can scale independently. The three growth labels are separate resource levers, not a requirement to increase them together. The enlarged shapes are conceptual capacity cues, not measured instance specifications or a performance graph.\n\nLatency: end-to-end and tail latency depend on the query plan, locks, CPU, memory/cache hits, concurrency, application networking, and storage. More CPU/RAM can help, and remote storage can still impose a network I/O path. Storage I/O: usable IOPS and throughput depend on the storage tier, configured performance, workload, and instance limits; a larger volume is not universally equivalent to more IOPS. gp3, Hyperdisk Balanced, and Premium SSD v2 provide configurable performance independently of capacity. High availability: existing managed providers already offer HA and automatic failover. Resizing alone does not establish an HA configuration or determine application interruption, recovery time, and connection handling. Persistent data and continuously available service are different requirements. Keep HA as a discovery topic beyond resource sizing, not an alleged missing competitor feature or a consequence caused solely by network storage.\n\nManaged services already automate operations such as backups, updates, monitoring, and failover according to their offering and configuration. Ask about application impact during maintenance, recovery tests, restores, connection handling, and how much work remains with the customer's team. The later PlanetScale architecture slide covers the operated HA platform; Metal's local-NVMe slide explains the storage path; benchmark and customer slides provide measured evidence. Do not claim that local NVMe alone solves HA or guarantees a speedup. Transition: “Convex is one production team that evaluated a different approach and moved from Aurora.”\n\nSources verified 2026-09-27: https://planetscale.com/blog/io-devices-and-latency ; https://planetscale.com/blog/benchmarking-postgres ; https://planetscale.com/blog/planetscale-metal-theres-no-replacement-for-displacement ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/CHAP_Storage.html ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/introduction ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/instance-settings ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/storage-options-overview ; https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/high-availability ; https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/postgresql/compute-storage/concepts-storage ; https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/postgresql/scale/concepts-scaling-resources . Research: docs/nexus-research.md. Gong structure: establish the buyer's current approach, challenge what adding capacity leaves unresolved, and run discovery before customer proof."),
 dict(id='vitalize',label='Vitalize: 2 ms p95, half the vCPUs',theme='vitalize',html=f'''<div class="case-heading vitalize-heading"><span class="vitalize-title-symbol"><img src="assets/vitalize-symbol.svg" alt=""></span><h2>Vitalize migrated from Supabase to Metal</h2></div><div class="vitalize-art">{vitalize_art}</div>''',notes='1:45. “Vitalize moved a 400 GB Postgres database with 150 million rows from Supabase to PlanetScale Metal. Their 200 GB JSONB-heavy table had reads averaging 50 seconds on Supabase; after the move, they reported reads maxing out at 1.2 seconds on PlanetScale. Those are different summary statistics—average versus maximum—not a like-for-like percentile benchmark. Do not calculate a precise speedup from them. On the same 16 GB RAM, they moved from four vCPUs on Supabase to two on PlanetScale. They report about 2 ms p95 after migration, but do not publish a Supabase p95 comparison. They also reported fewer than 150 usable connections and over 60% CPU time in I/O wait before the move; connections became manageable after. Their cutover involved scaling the app to zero while replication caught up, so do not claim an uninterrupted app cutover despite the blog headline. This is their reported experience, not a performance or migration guarantee. Ask which query, connection, or I/O limit most resembles the prospect’s workload. Transition: “Autumn shows how a team made the migration with PlanetScale.” Source: https://vitalize.care/blog/from-supabase-to-planetscale . Other cases: docs/customer-case-studies.md.'),
 dict(id='availability',label='Postgres Cluster Architecture',theme='availability',html=f'''<div class="title-with-stat"><h2>Postgres Cluster Architecture</h2><div class="sla"><strong>99.99<span>%</span></strong><p>single-region HA SLA</p></div></div><div class="ha-diagram">{ha}</div>''',notes="2:00. “This is one highly available Postgres cluster: three database nodes, each in a different availability zone within the same region. One node is primary; the other two are replicas.” A node (also called an instance in PlanetScale documentation) is an individual database server. An availability zone is the cloud location and failure boundary hosting that node. The whole deployment is a cluster, not one node. A, B, and C are schematic zone labels, not literal provider zone IDs. This depicts the standard three-node HA configuration; additional replicas are possible. Trace application connections, then replication, then operations. The two connection boxes are logical routing choices, not a claim of separate DNS endpoints. Replica reads are explicitly selected with a replica credential/username suffix on direct port 5432; reads must tolerate replication lag. Default PgBouncer connections on 6432 route to the primary; dedicated replica PgBouncers are a separate option. Semi-synchronous replication requires durable confirmation from at least one replica before commit success is returned. PlanetScale's custom Kubernetes operator manages the cluster's provisioning, health, failover, resizing, and upgrades; it is outside the SQL query path. Failovers can interrupt connections, so applications need retries. The 99.99% figure is the single-region monthly uptime commitment under the applicable SLA; it excludes single-node clusters and beta features. Hosted Postgres on Metal remains the offer. Transition: “Local NVMe is the hardware foundation. Let's look at its performance under load.” Verified 2026-09-27. Sources: https://planetscale.com/docs/postgres/postgres-architecture ; https://planetscale.com/docs/postgres/operations-philosophy ; https://planetscale.com/docs/postgres/scaling/replicas ; https://planetscale.com/legal/planetscale-service-level-agreement . Durable terminology: docs/postgres-architecture.md."),
 dict(id='metal',label='Postgres on Metal: throughput and p99',theme='metal',html=f'''<div class="metal-heading"><h2>More QPS &amp; Lower p99 on <span class="metal-title-brand"><span class="metal-title-symbol"><img src="assets/planetscale-white.svg" alt="PlanetScale"></span><span class="accent">Metal</span></span></h2></div><div class="benchmark-grid"><div><h3>Throughput (QPS)</h3>{bars}</div><div><h3>p99 latency</h3><div class="chart-legend"><span class="ps-dot">PlanetScale</span><span class="aurora-dot">Aurora</span><span class="alloydb-dot">AlloyDB</span><span class="supabase-dot">Supabase</span></div>{latency}</div></div>''',notes="1:45. “In this mixed read/write benchmark, Postgres on Metal delivers more throughput and lower p99 than these tested configurations. We can evaluate the same questions against your application.” Left: arithmetic mean QPS across 300 one-second samples with 32 concurrent connections: PlanetScale 16,338.6276; Aurora 10,908.9315; AlloyDB 10,355.0109; Supabase 5,270.2068. Right: one-second interval p99 samples from the same TPCC-like benchmark, not an aggregate run-wide percentile. PlanetScale ranges 170.48–223.34 ms; Aurora 325.98–733 ms; AlloyDB 277.21–1,235.62 ms; Supabase 196.89–1,561.52 ms. All four traces use the matching 32-connection run, with all 300 samples retained and a shared linear 0–1,200 ms axis. Values above 1,200 ms are visually clipped, not changed in the source data. Each provider was benchmarked separately; the horizontal axis is elapsed run time, not simultaneous wall-clock time. Explain the workload aloud: mixed reads and writes, about 500 GB, 32 concurrent connections, five minutes. Higher QPS and lower p99 are distinct findings.\n\nTESTED DATABASE MACHINES (per node)\n- PlanetScale Postgres on Metal: M-320 on AWS i8g; 4 vCPUs, 32 GB RAM, 937 GB local NVMe; AWS us-east-1. Published IOPS setting: unlimited (no separately provisioned IOPS tier, not infinite physical capacity).\n- Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL: db.r8g.xlarge; 4 vCPUs, 32 GB RAM; autoscaling Aurora storage and IOPS; AWS us-east-1.\n- Google AlloyDB: N2; 4 vCPUs, 32 GB RAM; autoscaling storage; IOPS listed as N/A; GCP us-central1.\n- Supabase: 2XLARGE; 8 vCPUs, 32 GB RAM, 750 GB provisioned EBS; IOPS increased to 12,000; AWS us-east-1. Supabase has twice the vCPUs to match the RAM, so the configurations are not identical.\n\nBENCHMARK CLIENT MACHINES\n- AWS tests: c6a.xlarge, 4 vCPUs and 8 GB RAM, in us-east-1.\n- GCP / AlloyDB test: e2-standard-4, 4 vCPUs and 16 GB RAM, in us-central1.\n- Clients run in the same region as their database. Same availability-zone placement is not guaranteed for this TPCC-like test.\n\nRUN SETTINGS\n- Percona sysbench TPCC-like mixed reads and writes; approximately 500 GB; TABLES=20, SCALE=250.\n- This slide selects 32 concurrent connections, 300 seconds, one-second reports, and p99.\n- PostgreSQL settings use provider defaults except connection limits/timeouts where needed for the benchmark.\n- PlanetScale has a primary and two HA replicas, each with the listed resources. The measured load uses the primary only; the graph does not sum throughput across three nodes.\n\nINTERPRETATION\n These are vendor-run synthetic results with different hardware and infrastructure, not isolated proof that NVMe causes every difference. The site's rounded ~18k headline spans other runs; this deck deliberately uses a single concurrency and exact samples. A separate read-only test has roughly equal average throughput for Aurora and PlanetScale, so do not claim every workload is faster by this ratio. Ask: “Which workload would we need to reproduce for this to matter to you?” Validate p95/p99, throughput, errors, and resource use in discovery. Transition: “When one machine becomes the constraint, Neki extends the architecture across Postgres shards.” Sources: https://planetscale.com/benchmarks ; https://planetscale.com/benchmarks/aurora ; https://planetscale.com/benchmarks/alloydb ; https://planetscale.com/benchmarks/supabase . Data definitions: docs/postgres-metal-benchmarks.md."),
 dict(id='neki',label='Neki: Horizontal Scaling for Postgres',theme='neki',html=f'''<div class="neki-heading"><h2>Neki: Horizontal Scaling for Postgres</h2><div class="neki-lockup"><img src="assets/neki-cat.svg" alt="Neki"></div></div><div class="neki-topology">{neki}</div>''',notes="1:15. “Neki is our sharded Postgres architecture. Every shard is real Postgres, with routing and operations managed as one database.” Walk from the application to the router, then one shard group, then the control plane. Sidecars pool connections; shard primaries and replicas carry data. The diagram represents replicated HA shard groups, not the primary-only benchmark on the next slide. Say that Neki is available in Platform Preview as of the verified documentation on 27 September 2026; it is not covered by an SLA. The public site's “now available” wording does not mean general availability. Cross-shard transactions remain listed as coming soon. Evaluate shard keys, query/transaction compatibility and the transition from the current database with the team. Do not imply an automatic in-place conversion from ordinary PlanetScale Postgres. The immediate sale remains hosted Postgres on Metal. Transition: “The benchmark demonstrates how far this architecture can scale for a defined workload.” Sources: https://neki.dev/ ; https://planetscale.com/docs/neki ; https://planetscale.com/blog/the-architecture-of-neki ."),
 dict(id='neki-scale',label='Neki: 118.5M queries per second',theme='neki neki-scale',html=f'''<div class="neki-heading"><h2><span class="yellow">118.5M</span> queries / second</h2><div class="neki-lockup"><img src="assets/neki-cat.svg" alt="Neki"></div></div><div class="scale-layout"><div><div class="scale-chart">{scale}</div><p class="neki-workload">Read-only point selects / Primary-only shards</p></div><div class="scale-metrics"><div><strong>1.22 <span>PiB</span></strong><p>data</p></div><div><strong>512</strong><p>shards</p></div><div><strong>6.06 <span>ms</span></strong><p>router p99</p></div></div></div>''',notes="1:00. “The published Neki run sustained 118.5 million queries per second across 512 shards over a 1.22 PiB dataset.” Follow the 5-, 50-, and 512-shard series and stop. Exact QPS: 999,624; 9,923,900; 118,538,803. Both axes are logarithmic. Workload: read-only, one-row primary-key point selects sent to a single shard. The measured configuration had 512 primary-only shards and 480 routers, without replicas or a failover event, for 16 minutes. It does not establish write performance or HA behavior. The 6.06 ms p99 is at the router; client p99 was 13.95 ms. These metrics do not share the TPCC workload or Vitalize workload and should not be compared on a common chart. Transition: “Convex shows how a production workload benefited from the move to PlanetScale.” Source: https://planetscale.com/blog/118-million-queries-per-second-on-neki ."),
 dict(id='convex',label='Convex migrated from Aurora to PlanetScale Postgres Metal',theme='customer-case convex',html=f'''<div class="case-heading convex-heading"><img class="convex-title-symbol" src="assets/convex-symbol-color.svg" alt=""><h2>Convex migrated from Aurora to PlanetScale Postgres Metal</h2></div><div class="convex-curves">{convex_plot}</div><div class="convex-quote"><q>p50 is the new p99.</q><span>Jamie Turner / Convex</span></div>''',notes="1:15. Convex provides backend infrastructure for developers. Its Chef product stores message histories and project snapshots in a Convex backend. The team moved that workload from AWS Aurora to PlanetScale for Postgres. Query p99 moved from a noisy 10–15 ms to a steadier 5–7 ms; batch-commit p99 had spiked between 75 and 200 ms and subsequently stayed around 20 ms. Both panels display the unchanged original customer screenshot, cropped only at the panel boundary through CSS. A CSS invert/hue-rotation filter adapts its light background to dark mode. All percentile lines, original axis values, and legends are preserved. The source chart vertical axes are in seconds; the numeric p99 summaries below are in milliseconds and use the article’s explicit reported ranges. The original query chart clips peaks above 0.02 seconds. Provider labels indicate the before/after portions around the visible step near 23:55, not a separately verified cutover timestamp. Each chart has its own vertical scale. The closing quote, “p50 is the new p99,” is Jamie Turner’s wording in the customer post. Treat it as a qualitative summary of the improvement, not a claim that every old p50 exactly equals every new p99. Preserve the approximate qualifier on 20 ms. Do not imply that all Convex workloads achieved these results, identify the prior Aurora engine without evidence, or claim a controlled hardware comparison. The article discusses Metal as the motivation but does not specify the measured cluster tier; label the destination PlanetScale for Postgres. Gong structure: infrastructure buyer's need, one named production workload, the change, and the measured outcome. Ask which tail-latency path matters most to the prospect. Source: https://news.convex.dev/powered-by-planetscale-for-postgres/ . Transition: “Autumn shows what the migration partnership can look like.”"),
 dict(id='autumn',label='Autumn: two days to PlanetScale Metal',theme='customer-case autumn',html=f'''<div class="case-heading autumn-heading"><div class="autumn-title-symbol"><img src="assets/autumn.svg" alt=""></div><h2>Autumn migrated to PlanetScale Metal in two days</h2></div><div class="autumn-evidence"><div>{autumn_plot}</div><div class="autumn-migration"><div class="migration-number"><strong>2<span> days</span></strong><p>Migration</p></div><div class="cutover-number"><strong>Seconds</strong><p>Cutover downtime</p></div><div class="migration-assist"><span class="migration-team-symbol"><img src="assets/planetscale-white.svg" alt="PlanetScale"></span><p>Migration team<br><span>Copy + cutover support</span></p></div></div></div>''',notes="1:15. Autumn provides billing infrastructure for AI companies, including real-time access and credit checks in the request path. Its previous provider had connection-pooling issues and inconsistent latency. The team expected migration to take weeks; it reports completing it in two days with seconds of cutover downtime. PlanetScale's team helped with replication and recommended its pgcopydb fork. General query latency moved from approximately 100 ms to consistently below 10 ms after the move to PlanetScale Metal. These values have no published percentile; do not call them p95 or p99. The previous provider is unnamed. A separate later support engagement involved query/index changes and a p99 improvement from around 200 ms to under 50 ms; that separate optimization is intentionally excluded from this slide. The white bar uses the approximate 100 ms baseline; the dashed orange bar depicts an upper bound of 10 ms, not a measured 10 ms value. Autumn’s original Insights screenshot is a later post-migration view, so it is not presented as the migration before/after comparison. The official current Autumn logo is monochrome; no invented color version is used. Timings are Autumn's experience, not a migration promise. Gong structure: business-critical workload, concerns about switching, assisted migration, observed result. Transition to technical discovery and migration assessment: assess the prospect's dependencies, workload, and cutover needs. Source: https://useautumn.com/blog/migrating-to-planetscale . Transition: “Let’s map what a migration would involve for your team.”"),
 dict(id='migration',label='Migrate to PlanetScale',theme='migration',html=f'''<h2>Migrate to PlanetScale</h2><div class="migration-diagram">{migration}</div><div class="migration-paths" role="group" aria-label="Migration options"><div class="migration-path self-service"><div class="migration-path-heading"><h3>Self-service migration</h3></div></div><span class="migration-option-slash" aria-hidden="true">/</span><div class="migration-path planetscale-led"><div class="migration-path-heading"><span class="migration-team-symbol"><img src="assets/planetscale-white.svg" alt=""></span><h3>PlanetScale-led migration</h3></div></div></div><div class="migration-close"><strong>Technical discovery + migration assessment</strong></div>''',notes="2:00. “You can move your existing Postgres to PlanetScale Postgres on Metal in either of two ways. Your team can run the migration with our assessment tooling, guides, and scripts. Or our migration specialists can lead the work with your team, from a custom plan through setup, data transfer, validation, and cutover.” Self-service is an engineering-led process, not a universal one-click importer. PlanetScale documents dump and restore, WAL streaming using logical replication, and pgcopydb, with provider-specific approaches where needed. Match the method to the source, data size, permissions, and downtime requirements. For PlanetScale-led work, agree on responsibilities, scope, timing, and pricing through the assessment. Migration services are not restricted to Enterprise customers; do not promise free services or a fixed delivery timeline. The shared flow shows a destination, not a single migration mechanism for every source. In technical discovery, assess schemas, extensions, write rate, auth dependencies, connection patterns, networking, and recovery requirements. Define representative p95/p99 and throughput targets. Depending on the method, the source can remain active during data copy and logical replication. Rehearse and validate before final synchronization and application cutover; downtime and rollback depend on the workload and target-side writes. Close: “Let’s bring your database lead into a technical session to assess the workload and agree on the right migration approach.” Establish owners and a meeting date. Autumn is a customer example of a guided migration, not a service guarantee. Sources verified 2026-09-27: https://planetscale.com/migrate ; https://planetscale.com/docs/postgres/imports/postgres-migrate-walstream ; https://planetscale.com/docs/postgres/imports/discovery-tool . Durable context: docs/migration-options.md. Working nine-slide core pitch: about 12.75 minutes before buyer exchanges."),
 ]
# Local and remote data I/O paths; durability is covered on the HA slide.
slides.append(dict(id='metal-path', label='Why Metal: Postgres on local NVMe', theme='metal-path', html='<h2>Why <span class="accent">Metal</span>: local NVMe</h2><div class="metal-path-art">'+(R/'assets/metal-path.svg').read_text()+'</div>', notes="1:00. “The difference is where the drive lives. With EBS, Postgres runs on EC2 compute and disk I/O travels over the EBS storage network to a separate SSD volume. Metal keeps the CPU, memory, and local NVMe SSDs on the same physical host. That removes the storage network hop and provides more I/O headroom without a separately provisioned IOPS tier.” Trace the long external path on the left and the local path inside the orange host on the right. The latency figures come from the opening animation in Ben Dicken’s March 13, 2025 article: local NVMe ~20 µs, EBS io2 Block Express ~400 µs (0.4 ms), and EBS gp3 ~1 ms (1,000 µs). They are hard-coded illustrative storage I/O durations, not benchmark measurements, measured averages, query latencies, or service guarantees. gp3 is the general-purpose SSD tier; io2 Block Express is the premium provisioned-IOPS tier with lower and more consistent latency. AWS describes gp3 as single-digit millisecond latency and io2 Block Express as average under 500 µs for 16 KiB I/O on Nitro instances. Ben’s article separately uses 50 µs local versus 250 µs generic network SSD later; it does not reconcile the two illustrations. Preserve the tier-specific labels; The displayed ratios are arithmetic on these illustrative estimates: 400/20 = 20 and 1,000/20 = 50. They describe lower illustrative storage I/O latency, not query speedups, measured Metal averages, or guaranteed results.  The AWS diagram is a simplified view of data I/O, with Nitro virtualization, operating system, filesystem, and cache layers omitted. EBS on Nitro can also appear as an NVMe device; the differentiator is local physical storage, not the device protocol name alone. EBS performance is bounded by both volume performance and the EC2 instance's EBS limits. EBS-optimized instances use dedicated EBS bandwidth; do not describe this as ordinary internet traffic or automatically competing with application networking. PlanetScale Metal runs on storage-optimized cloud hardware; the product name does not imply a dedicated bare-metal server for every customer. Local SSDs have finite throughput and capacity. The absence of a provisioned IOPS tier does not mean infinite IOPS. Do not promise a fixed speedup or attribute the complete provider benchmark result solely to storage. Ask: “Are I/O wait, provisioned IOPS, or p99 stalls constraining your database?” Transition: “Here are the results for one published workload; let's assess yours.” HA and durability are covered on the preceding architecture slide. Sources: https://planetscale.com/blog/io-devices-and-latency ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/general-purpose.html ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/provisioned-iops.html ; https://planetscale.com/metal ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/InstanceStorage.html ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ebs-optimized.html ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/nvme-ebs-volumes.html ."))


# Optional cloud/account discovery slide immediately before the migration close.
slides.append(dict(id='cloud', label='Choose how you deploy PlanetScale', theme='cloud-offerings', html='<h2>Choose how you deploy <span class="accent">PlanetScale</span></h2>\n<div class="cloud-providers" role="group" aria-label="Supported clouds">\n  <img class="cloud-aws-logo" src="assets/aws.svg" alt="AWS">\n  <span class="cloud-provider-slash" aria-hidden="true">/</span>\n  <div class="cloud-google-lockup"><img src="assets/google-cloud-symbol.png" alt=""><span>Google Cloud</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class="cloud-deployments" role="group" aria-label="Cloud deployment options">\n  <div class="cloud-deployment hosted">\n    <h3>PlanetScale-hosted</h3>\n    <div class="cloud-account"><p class="cloud-account-label">PlanetScale account</p><div class="cloud-database"><img src="assets/planetscale-symbol-white.svg" alt="PlanetScale"><span>Postgres</span></div></div>\n  </div>\n  <span class="cloud-option-slash" aria-hidden="true">/</span>\n  <div class="cloud-deployment byoc">\n    <h3>Your cloud account <span>(BYOC)</span></h3>\n    <div class="cloud-account"><p class="cloud-account-label">Your AWS account / GCP project</p><div class="cloud-database"><img src="assets/planetscale-symbol-white.svg" alt="PlanetScale"><span>Postgres</span></div><p class="cloud-enterprise">Enterprise · PlanetScale Managed</p></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<div class="cloud-operations"><strong>Database operations managed by PlanetScale</strong></div>\n<div class="cloud-connectivity" role="group" aria-label="Private connectivity options, setup by PlanetScale and your team">\n  <div class="cloud-connectivity-heading"><strong>Private connectivity options</strong><span class="cloud-connectivity-setup">Setup: PlanetScale + your team</span></div>\n  <p>AWS PrivateLink <span class="cloud-connectivity-slash" aria-hidden="true">/</span> GCP Private Service Connect</p>\n</div>', notes="0:45, optional discovery slide. Discovery prompts: “Which region does your application run in?” “Do you require dedicated infrastructure or ownership of the cloud account?” “Must database traffic stay on private networks?” Use the answers to guide the account choice and network discussion. “PlanetScale runs on AWS and Google Cloud. You can use our hosted service, or choose PlanetScale Managed so the database runs in an account or project your company owns. In either case, our team operates the database.” Match deployment to the answer and move to migration; do not read a region catalog aloud. BYOC means Bring Your Own Cloud: the customer owns the AWS account or GCP project and PlanetScale operates the database. It is an Enterprise deployment model. Deployment scope verified 2026-09-27: the current official deployment and Managed documentation lists AWS/GCP account deployments and does not document a supported deployment on customer-owned on-premises hardware. Do not offer on-premises hosting or ongoing operation of an existing on-premises database as a PlanetScale service. An on-premises Postgres source can be assessed for migration to a supported PlanetScale cloud deployment, using the documented import methods where compatible; confirm permissions, networking, extensions and cutover needs. The Managed overview uses the phrase on-premises in marketing prose, but its deployment instructions specify an AWS member account or GCP project; that wording alone is not evidence of an on-premises product. PlanetScale Managed supports Postgres, Vitess and Neki; this slide focuses on Postgres. For AWS BYOC, the data plane runs in a dedicated AWS Organizations member account owned by the customer and operated by PlanetScale. For GCP it runs in a dedicated customer-owned project. The PlanetScale control plane remains outside that customer organization; avoid saying every component lives in the customer's account. Hosted single-tenancy is also an Enterprise option; keep it for technical discovery when isolation matters. The dotted band applies to database operations in either deployment model. The separate neutral row names private connectivity options and labels them Setup: PlanetScale + your team. This label describes the setup responsibilities documented for PlanetScale Managed, verified 2026-09-27. Talk track: “We configure the database-side service and authorize your account or project. Your network team configures the private endpoint and network settings where your application runs.” PlanetScale configures the service side, and the customer configures the application-side endpoint and network. With AWS PrivateLink, PlanetScale provisions the endpoint service and cross-account authorization, and the customer creates the interface endpoint in the application VPC with the chosen subnets and security groups. With GCP Private Service Connect, PlanetScale provides the service attachment and allowlists application projects, and the customer configures the consumer endpoint, VPC, subnet and private IP. Explain the ownership split aloud when networking is relevant. Confirm deployment, product and configuration eligibility, endpoint setup and network requirements before proposing either. Hosted private connections can also be available; BYOC is not a prerequisite in every supported configuration. BYOC does not automatically mean private-only endpoints. Confirm region, storage tier, CPU architecture and size availability for the prospect's selected configuration, including local NVMe hardware if proposing Metal. Do not promise that every Metal size is available in every provider region. Region reference verified 2026-09-27: the general platform catalog lists AWS Tokyo, Mumbai, Singapore, Sydney, Montreal, Frankfurt, Dublin, London, Sao Paulo, Northern Virginia, Ohio and Oregon; GCP Iowa, Northern Virginia, Montreal, Seoul, South Carolina, Belgium and the Netherlands. This is a platform catalog, not a guarantee of every Postgres/Metal SKU in each location. Enterprise may offer additional regions with at least three availability zones and required services; validate hardware and service eligibility with Solutions. The region closest to the application is the starting point; assess residency and private networking needs. Infrastructure billing for BYOC goes to the customer's cloud account, with a separate PlanetScale management charge; do not show an unverified price. Transition: “Let's assess the right deployment and migration approach with your database lead.” Sources: https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/deployment-options ; https://planetscale.com/docs/postgres/imports/postgres-migrate-dumprestore ; https://planetscale.com/postgres ; https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/managed ; https://planetscale.com/enterprise ; https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/managed/aws ; https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/managed/gcp ; https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/regions ; https://planetscale.com/pricing ; https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/managed/aws/privatelink ; https://planetscale.com/docs/plans/managed/gcp/private-service-connect . Original AWS logo: https://aws.amazon.com/ (navigation SVG). Original Google Cloud icon: https://www.gstatic.com/images/branding/product/2x/google_cloud_64dp.png ."))

# Keep the customer proof together, then show architecture, performance, future scale, and migration.
slide_order=['opening','nexus','convex','vitalize','autumn','availability','metal-path','metal','neki','neki-scale','cloud','migration']
slides_by_id={slide['id']:slide for slide in slides}
assert set(slide_order)==set(slides_by_id) and len(slide_order)==len(slides)
slides=[slides_by_id[slide_id] for slide_id in slide_order]
notes_fixes={

 'convex': [('Transition: “Autumn shows what the migration partnership can look like.”','Transition: “Autumn shows how a team made the migration with PlanetScale.”')],
 'autumn': [('Transition: “Let’s map what a migration would involve for your team.”','Transition: “Here is the architecture behind the managed Postgres platform.”')],
 'metal': [('Transition: “When one machine becomes the constraint, Neki extends the architecture across Postgres shards.”','Transition: “When one machine becomes the constraint, Neki extends the architecture across Postgres shards.”')],
 'neki-scale': [('Transition: “Convex shows how a production workload benefited from the move to PlanetScale.”','Transition: “Let’s map what a migration would involve for your team.”')],
}
for slide in slides:
 for old,new in notes_fixes.get(slide['id'],[]):
  assert old in slide['notes'],(slide['id'],old)
  slide['notes']=slide['notes'].replace(old,new)
(R/'slides.js').write_text('window.PITCH_BRAND = '+json.dumps((R/'assets/planetscale-white.svg').read_text())+';\nwindow.PITCH_SLIDES = '+json.dumps(slides,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+';\n')
(R/'qa/content.json').write_text(json.dumps([{'id':s['id'],'label':s['label']} for s in slides],indent=2))
(R/'qa/links.json').write_text('{}\n')
(R/'speaker-notes.md').write_text('# PlanetScale Postgres — speaker notes\n\nTen-slide core deck · Optional Vitalize story: ?vitalize · Optional cloud deployment slide: ?cloud (before migration)\n\n'+'\n\n'.join(f'## {i}. {s["label"]}\n\n{s["notes"]}' for i,s in enumerate(slides,1))+'\n')
print(f'Built {len(slides)} slides, vector diagrams and speaker notes.')
